WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Give Claude to two people. One will use

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.960
it to build a better email. The other

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.920
will use it to help build a hundred

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.400
million dollar company. Same tool,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.320
different users.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:19.640
And that gap between people who use AI

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.800
and people who wield it is about to

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:26.760
reshape careers and companies and

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.400
families and lives over the next decade.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.480
If you're new here, I've been a CEO,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.760
board member, investor in technology

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.080
companies worth billions. And today I

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:40.240
want to share with you how to use Claude

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.520
better than 99% of people, from beginner

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.440
to advanced. And it's way easier than

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:48.480
you think. Claude is not just one app,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.435
it's a whole stack of tools built for a

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.120
very different kind of work environment.

00:00:50.435 --> 00:00:53.440
[music]

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.240
For businesses, there's chat for

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.200
thinking, Claude co-work to get work

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.720
done, Claude code to build things,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.190
Claude Chrome to browse with a brain,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.280
Claude skills to make it all repeatable,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.160
[music] and lots of tools. Once you

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.360
understand how this team of agents work

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.800
together, your output can change

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.640
dramatically. The best way to visualize

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.560
it is this: five activities, think,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.480
remember, execute, build, and browse.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.640
Let's go through each one of them. First

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.880
is think. You go to Claude chat. And

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.440
this is what most of us do when we think

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.480
about chatbots. We input a prompt, we

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:34.320
get a response. You bring a messy

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.640
problem, a fuzzy idea, or a rough draft

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.520
and work through it. But many of us just

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.160
use AI to do all the work so they can

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.840
copy and paste. We have all seen those

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:49.480
LinkedIn posts. So, if you go to Claude

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.880
chat and type write me a LinkedIn post

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:55.040
about leadership, you'll get a very

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.240
polished paragraph that says nothing.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.211
And we've talked about how to prompt

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.760
better with things like aim before

00:01:59.211 --> 00:02:04.400
[music] actor input mission. Try this

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.400
prompt instead. You are a business

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.880
school professor. I am trying to explain

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.280
why some leaders sound credible and

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.600
others don't. I'm attaching my notion

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.720
page with a rough idea that talks about

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.959
leadership and authenticity, leadership

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:25.959
and humility. Your job is to push back

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:28.320
on what's weak and sharpen what's real.

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:31.306
Find me research that helps me create a

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.240
strong insight and then we can write

00:02:31.306 --> 00:02:35.240
[music]

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.840
a LinkedIn post. You make Claude your

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.640
thinking partner now. Of course, like

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.560
all AI, it will not do what you tell it

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.000
to do, not the first time around at

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.880
least. And sometimes it's going to be

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.320
super frustrating because you will give

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.320
this detailed prompt and just crap will

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.480
come back. So, there are three things

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.480
that you can do [music]

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.040
that can become your power moves when

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.519
that happens. First, give it rich

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.040
context as much as you can. Connect the

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:05.720
chatbot to your Google Drive, your

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.400
email, your calendar, your notion app,

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.880
anything else you can. That way you can

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.680
attach files to that chat session. It

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.239
will read it, it'll get context [music]

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.720
from it. Second, ground your insights

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:20.080
with research. You can ask Claude to go

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:22.680
out on the web and do deep research. It

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.760
has already learned the entire internet

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.600
during its training, so why not use it?

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.040
But when you get the research back,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.160
always ask one more time, is this

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:37.000
verified? And third, use AI to build

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.560
your AI skill in real time. If you are

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.720
patient and curious, you can literally

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.920
build your engine while you're flying

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.959
the plane. For instance, let's say you

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.040
have no background in corporate finance

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:51.000
and you're building a financial model.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:53.640
You get stuck on how to use a specific

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.480
spreadsheet [music] feature or even a

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.560
financial concept like IRR or free cash

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.120
flow.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.880
No problem. Just start another chat in

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:07.240
another tab and ask away. So, you can

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:09.959
learn and do. Do and learn. The cycle

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.160
just continues. Now, let's go meet our

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:14.720
second member of your team.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.359
This one helps you remember and it's

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:21.239
called Claude Projects. We all know when

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:23.640
we're creating something new or solving

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:27.080
a complicated problem, the work doesn't

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:29.760
end [music] in one session or even in

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:32.480
one chat. And that's why all three AI

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.280
platforms nowadays have gotten better at

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.919
maintaining memory across different chat

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.400
sessions. Claude remembers a world

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.560
through projects.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:45.440
A project is where you give the chatbot

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.280
your files, your instructions, your

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.840
tone, your ongoing work. You're not

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:52.133
starting from zero every time you open

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.320
it. And of course,

00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:54.200
>> [music]

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.000
>> I would be careful about sharing

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:59.480
confidential documents from your office,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.360
but let's say you're actively or

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:05.120
passively looking for a career change or

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.919
a new job opportunity. You can create a

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.560
project. You drop in your resume, your

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:12.400
LinkedIn profile, the two or three job

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.720
descriptions that you like or the

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.240
company you're targeting, your writing

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.680
samples, your professional bio, the

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:21.760
other resumes that you like, anything

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.840
you can think of. And you can customize

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.080
this project with exactly what you need.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.200
So, you can add something like, "You're

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.320
the world's most badass [music]

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.360
recruiter and resume writer who has

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.240
placed candidates in top companies in my

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.840
industry. I am a brand marketer with 5

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:44.320
years of experience at a large agency

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:47.240
called WPP. Your job is to help me

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.720
create a great story for my next

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:52.760
adventure, for my next company, and make

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:55.400
me desirable for the next role. I write

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:57.200
in a direct, confident tone. I don't

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.600
like corporate fillers. I hate

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.080
buzzwords. When I ask you to tailor

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.960
something, match the job description's

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.800
language without losing my voice. When I

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.079
ask you to do deep research, make sure

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.840
you deliver only the relevant and

00:06:10.079 --> 00:06:15.040
[music] verified data. Don't make it up.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.919
And when I ask you for feedback, be

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.200
honest. Tell me what's weak [music] and

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:23.000
what I need to work on. Be my

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.880
constructive partner and strengthen

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.080
those areas. This is one way to provide

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.680
instructions, but you can see that there

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.320
are 100 ways to do it. And that's the

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.480
kind of instruction that makes any

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.279
project your own. Not a prompt that

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.280
you're copying from some YouTuber,

00:06:36.279 --> 00:06:40.800
[music]

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.829
but a brief that you write for someone

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.919
you actually know very well,

00:06:42.829 --> 00:06:44.560
>> [music]

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:46.800
>> yourself.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.520
From that point on, every time you come

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.689
back, Claude already knows what you want

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:50.800
and who you are.

00:06:50.689 --> 00:06:53.320
>> [music]

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.800
>> You can ask it to tailor a cover letter,

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:59.440
craft a specific bullet of your resume

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:02.560
for a specific role. A project turns

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.099
Claude from a one-off conversation

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.360
into an ongoing relationship between

00:07:05.099 --> 00:07:11.280
[music] you and your AI. This is also

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:14.240
why I don't customize Claude at the

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.800
account level. I like customizing it at

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.680
the [music] project level, because that

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:21.760
way I can give it specific context.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:24.480
Because using AI to plan your vacation

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.560
is very different from using it to plan

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.200
your retirement. Now, let's go to

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.600
execute. And for that, we have our third

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:35.480
team member, Claude co-work. Chat is

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:38.000
where the work begins. Co-work [music]

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.560
is where it gets done. You start in a

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.040
prompt window, but finish it on your

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.560
desktop. Co-work [music] is a desktop

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.480
application for Mac or Windows.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:51.000
Unfortunately, it's available only on

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.680
Claude's paid plans right now, but with

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.480
Claude Work, instead of asking a

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.600
question, you give it a task. When do

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.520
you use Claude Work versus chat? Well,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.600
you use Claude Work when you have a lot

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.080
of assets already on your computer's

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.600
hard drive, when the task needs multiple

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.920
steps, and when you need that output to

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.960
be on your local drive as a local file.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:13.280
So, for example, you can tell Claude

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.919
Work to do the [music] following. Take

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.960
all of these customer interview

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:20.360
transcripts from this folder, these

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:23.080
spreadsheets of accounts, and notes from

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:25.840
last week's product meeting, find top

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:28.200
three reasons on why the customers are

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.800
churning. Why are they leaving? Provide

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.520
all the supporting data, create a very

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.800
clean slide deck that I can use

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.560
tomorrow. Put the slide deck in this

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.760
folder. [music]

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:40.120
And here's what makes Claude Work even

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:43.159
more interesting. It can reach out to

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:47.400
other AI tools through a connector

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:51.000
called MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.800
Think of MCP as giving Claude a set of

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.200
keys [music]

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:56.760
to other AI tools. It can knock on their

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.800
door, unlock the capabilities of those

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.120
tools, act with them, act through them

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:03.120
to give you results. [music]

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:05.920
Let me show you what that means in a

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:09.000
real example. Imagine you run a business

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:11.000
selling handcrafted candles. You have a

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.920
small business. You have a terrific

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.680
product, but you're bootstrapping your

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:17.680
business, right? And you don't have a

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:20.280
huge marketing budget. In the old world,

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.800
that would mean that you couldn't create

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.920
any ads that looked super polished or

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.600
professional. But now, look at what you

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:30.560
can do when you can plug Claude into a

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.920
creative AI tool called Hicksfield. They

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.760
are the sponsor for this video. Now,

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:38.600
Hicksfield is used by 18 million the

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.680
backed by tier one VCs like Excel and

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:42.960
Menlo Ventures, and they've already

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.600
crossed the billion-dollar valuation.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.480
And they were the first creative

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.480
platform to ship an official MCP

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:52.040
connector for Claude. Here's how you

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:54.800
connect it. You have to do it once, and

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:58.080
that's it. Go to settings, connectors,

00:09:54.800 --> 00:10:00.600
plus button, paste the Hexfield URL, and

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.600
you're done. That's it. Now you can go

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:05.000
into Co-work, and you tell it to do

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:07.320
whatever you need to sell more products.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:11.000
For instance, you could say, "Generate

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:13.480
20 beautiful ad creatives for my new

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:16.920
lavender candle line. Three ideas for

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:19.360
Instagram, three editorial close-ups for

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:21.800
the website, and a vertical video for

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.120
the launch. Write a tagline for each."

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.222
And that's it.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:24.440
It just runs.

00:10:24.222 --> 00:10:27.280
>> [music]

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:29.760
>> Claude Co-work writes the prompt and

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.160
passes it on to Hexfield. Hexfield

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:35.120
generates the images and the video, and

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.920
the entire set of files land in your

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:40.720
local folder without you needing to hire

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.562
a creative agency and pay them thousands

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.960
of dollars. That would have taken you

00:10:42.562 --> 00:10:48.080
[music] 3 weeks of a professional shoot,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.839
a photographer, a stylist, and weeks of

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.520
post-production, and of course thousands

00:10:49.839 --> 00:10:56.520
of dollars. But now, you can do it in a

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:59.360
single session with Claude and Hexfield.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:02.200
This is how AI is leveling the playing

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.760
field. A solo founder with the right set

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:07.120
of tools today can now produce the kind

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:10.040
of creative output that used to require

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.320
a large team and a large budget. There's

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:14.480
a link in the description below. They

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:17.000
have a free tier. Take it for a spin.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:19.320
Try it. And you could build something

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:22.080
very cool and interesting this weekend.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.920
Two things to keep in mind. First, the

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.960
output will be a starting point. You'll

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:30.120
still have to go through each and every

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.880
slide and make sure it's good. But a lot

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.720
of heavy lifting is done by Claude

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.320
co-worker already. Second, give explicit

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:41.080
permission for Claude co-work to access

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:43.520
only specific directory and specific

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:46.400
files. Claude is very conscious about

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:48.600
security, but please be very mindful

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:51.120
about what you give access to. And I

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:54.080
usually create a subfolder and move all

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.480
the files and data into that folder, and

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.720
that's all Claude has access to. It's

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.480
called sandboxing, so the rest of your

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:03.128
hard drive remains secure. Claude can't

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:03.160
get to it. Fourth

00:12:03.128 --> 00:12:06.080
>> [music]

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.760
>> is build, and this is where Claude code

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.600
comes into play. This is the part that

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:15.320
makes a lot of us nervous. It made me

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:16.520
nervous. The moment we hear code, we're

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:17.880
like,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:20.400
"Ah, I don't know."

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:23.360
We assume that all that stuff is only

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:26.040
for engineers, but that assumption is

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:28.640
one of the biggest missed opportunities

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.720
in AI right now. If you can type in

